items.statement:
In the situation of Lemma 2.4.4(1), let I = J = Z_{≥0}, with transition maps M_{i,j} → M_{i+1,j} (for the colimit) and M_{i,j+1} → M_{i,j} (for the limit). Suppose we are given maps T′: M_{i,j} → M_{i−1,j+1} satisfying three conditions:
(a) the composite M_{i,j} → M_{i+1,j} →^{T′} M_{i,j+1} equals M_{i,j} →^{T′} M_{i−1,j+1} → M_{i,j+1};
(b) the composite M_{i+1,j} →^{T′} M_{i,j+1} → M_{i,j} equals M_{i+1,j} → M_{i+1,j−1} →^{T′} M_{i,j};
(c) T on M_{i,j} is the composite M_{i,j} → M_{i+1,j} →^{T′} M_{i,j+1} → M_{i,j}.
Then all transition maps and all T′ become quasi-isomorphisms on ordinary parts, and M^ord ≅ M_{i,j}^ord for every (i, j).

Setting of §2.5:
- S = Spec A, with A a finitely generated Z-algebra;
- (C, X, p₁, p₂) is a correspondence over S (C and X separated of finite type, p₁ × p₂: C → X × X proper);
- F is a coherent sheaf on X and T: p₂*F → p₁^!F is a cohomological correspondence, i.e. a map in D(O_{C,■});
- C = (C̄, X̄, p̄₁, p̄₂, D₊, D₋) is a strict dynamic compactification: a compactification of the correspondence with X̄ ∖ X = D₊ ∪ D₋, p̄₂*D₊ ≤ s·p̄₁*D₊ and p̄₁*D₋ ≤ s·p̄₂*D₋ for some 0 < s < 1;
- ordinary parts are taken for the endomorphisms T_C of Proposition 2.2.10.
Then RΓ_{D₊,D₋}(X̄, F)^ord and RΓ_{D₋,D₊}(X̄, F)^ord are canonically quasi-isomorphic.

In the setting of §2.5, assume D₊ and D₋ are locally principal (allowed by Proposition 2.2.11 and Lemma 2.1.30), and let F̄ be a coherent extension of F to X̄. After replacing D₊ and D₋ by nD₊ and nD₋ for a suitable common n ∈ Z_{>0}, there is an integer m such that, for all k₊ ≥ m and k₋ ≥ m, T extends to a map T: p̄₂*F̄(k₊D₊ − k₋D₋) → p̄₁^!F̄(k₊D₊ − k₋D₋). This map factors as the composite of three maps:
(1) the natural map p̄₂*F̄(k₊D₊ − k₋D₋) → p̄₂*F̄((k₊+1)D₊ − k₋D₋) (one more pole along D₊);
(2) a diagonal map p̄₂*F̄((k₊+1)D₊ − k₋D₋) → p̄₁^!F̄(k₊D₊ − (k₋+1)D₋);
(3) the natural map p̄₁^!F̄(k₊D₊ − (k₋+1)D₋) → p̄₁^!F̄(k₊D₊ − k₋D₋) (one zero fewer along D₋).

Suppose (C, X, p₁, p₂) admits a dynamic (resp. strict dynamic, resp. compact dynamic) compactification (C̄, X̄, p̄₁, p̄₂, D₊, D₋). Then for every n ≥ 1, (C^{(n)}, C^{(n−1)}, p₁^{(n)}, p₂^{(n)}) admits a dynamic (resp. strict dynamic, resp. compact dynamic) compactification, namely (C̄^{(n)}, C̄^{(n−1)}, p̄₁^{(n)}, p̄₂^{(n)}, D₊^{(n−1)}, D₋^{(n−1)}). Here:
- C̄^{(0)} = X̄, C̄^{(1)} = C̄, and C̄^{(n)} = C̄^{(n−1)} ×_{p̄₁^{(n−1)}, C̄^{(n−2)}, p̄₂^{(n−1)}} C̄^{(n−1)};
- D±^{(0)} = D±;
- D₊^{(n)} = (p̄₁^{(n)})*D₊^{(n−1)} and D₋^{(n)} = (p̄₂^{(n)})*D₋^{(n−1)}, as closed subschemes of C̄^{(n)}.

Let X be a scheme (of finite type over Z, hence quasi-compact and quasi-separated). Let Z_{−1} = ∅ ⊆ Z₀ ⊆ Z₁ ⊆ ⋯ ⊆ Z_d = X be a finite filtration by closed subschemes. Let î_l : Ẑ_l ↪ X be the corresponding closed subsets of the locale. Let U_l = X ∖ Z_l with open immersion j_l : U_l ↪ X, so U_{−1} = X and U_d = ∅. For F ∈ D(𝒪_{X,■}) put:
RΓ(Ẑ_l, F) := RΓ(X, (î_l)_⋆î_l^⋆F);
RΓ_c(U_l, F) := RΓ(X, (j_l)_!j_l^⋆F);
RΓ_c(Ẑ_l ∖ Ẑ_{l−1}, F) := RΓ(X, (j_{l−1})_!(j_{l−1})^⋆(î_l)_⋆(î_l)^⋆F).
H^n_c(Ẑ_l ∖ Ẑ_{l−1}, F) is the n-th cohomology of the last complex. The localization triangles give exact triangles RΓ_c(U_l, F) → RΓ(X, F) → RΓ(Ẑ_l, F) →(+1).

items.note:
Missing. Its ingredients are planned in AnalyticStacks:
- the base change p₂^⋆(j_l)_!j_l^⋆ = (j_{C_{U_l,2}})_! j^⋆_{C_{U_l,2}} p₂^⋆, from AS.0;
- the map induced by the inclusion C_{U_l,2} ⊆ C_{U_l,1};
- the counit (p′₁)_!(p′₁)^! → Id, from AS.3's formalism on schemes.
The construction itself is planned nowhere. It restricts a cohomological correspondence to the open complements of a contracted closed subset, and extends the result to the third terms of the localization triangles. The Part II of AutomorphicBundles proposed for Pilloni's 2020 coherent correspondences asks for 'restriction to opens and to locally closed strata' and 'base change to formal completions' of cohomological correspondences. That is what this construction provides.

Missing. It needs three things:
- the Cousin spectral sequence of Theorem 2.9.7, which is missing;
- cohomological correspondences for solid quasi-coherent sheaves, which are planned nowhere;
- the trace (p₁)_!p₁^! → Id for proper p₁. AnalyticStacks AS.3's formalism supplies this as a counit, since proper maps form its class P and so (p₁)_! = (p₁)_⋆.
The paper does not state that p₁ is proper, but it holds in the application. In the paper the proposition gives the action of t ∈ T^{++}(Q_p) on the Cousin spectral sequence of §4.5 (p. 52), together with Corollary 4.2.10.

Missing. It needs:
- compact dynamic compactifications (Definitions 2.1.19–2.1.26);
- the operator T_𝒞 of Proposition 2.2.10;
- finite rank (Definition 2.4.5: a factorisation through a complex with finite cohomology);
- Lemma 2.10.2 and the finiteness of Lemma 2.10.1(4).
None of these is planned. This is an algebraic finiteness condition. It is not the Banach-space compactness planned in LocallyAnalyticDistributions L4 and PadicFamilies L2a. Pilloni's GSp4 paper (Duke 2020) uses instead the compactness of the Klingen operator U on overconvergent cohomology (Corollary 13.2.4.1), which is proposed for the same Part II. Here finite rank serves Lemma 2.4.6: over an Artinian local ring the ordinary part has finite cohomology.

