In the setting of §2.11, let Y = X̄ ∖ (D′₊)°, the closure of X̄ ∖ D′₊ in X̄, with the scheme structure of the scheme-theoretic closure of X̄ ∖ D′₊ (as in Lemma 2.10.2). Put RΓ_c(X̄ ∖ ((D′₊)° ∪ D₋), F″) := RΓ_{∅,D₋}(Y, F″) = RΓ_{D₋,∅}(Y, F″), the cohomology of F″ on Y with compact support towards D₋; note X̄ ∖ ((D′₊)° ∪ D₋) ⊆ X″. There is a commutative diagram in which:
- the change-of-support maps RΓ_{D″₊,D₋}(X̄, F″) → RΓ_{D′₊,D₋}(X̄, F″) and RΓ_{D₋,D″₊}(X̄, F″) → RΓ_{D₋,D′₊}(X̄, F″) both factor through RΓ_c(X̄ ∖ ((D′₊)° ∪ D₋), F″). As in the proof of Lemma 2.10.2, the maps into the middle term are restriction to Y (Lemma 2.2.7; D″₊ ∩ Y = ∅ since D″₊ ⊆ (D′₊)°). The maps out of it are the change of support of Lemma 2.2.6 on Y, from (∅, D₋ ∩ Y) to (D′₊ ∩ Y, D₋ ∩ Y), resp. from (D₋ ∩ Y, ∅) to (D₋ ∩ Y, D′₊ ∩ Y), followed by the identifications with RΓ_{D′₊,D₋}(X̄, F″), resp. RΓ_{D₋,D′₊}(X̄, F″). These identifications use the scheme structure chosen on Y;
- there are maps T : RΓ_{D′₊,D₋}(X̄, F″) → RΓ_{D″₊,D₋}(X̄, F″) and T : RΓ_{D₋,D′₊}(X̄, F″) → RΓ_{D₋,D″₊}(X̄, F″), constructed as in Proposition 2.10.4(2) using p̄₂^⋆D′₊ ⊆ p̄₁^⋆D″₊ and p̄₁^⋆D₋ ⊆ p̄₂^⋆D₋;
- going around the diagram induces the endomorphism T of each term.
Consequently RΓ_{D″₊,D₋}(X̄, F″)^{ord} = RΓ_{D₋,D″₊}(X̄, F″)^{ord}.

