items.statement:
Let S^tor be the toroidal compactification of level K^pK_{p,n} of §4.1, after the blow-up of Proposition 4.1.13, viewed as an adic space over Spa(Q_p, Z_p) with formal model 𝔖^tor. Here K_{p,n} ⊆ G(Z_p) is the Iwahori subgroup of elements reducing mod p^n into B(Z/p^nZ). For 1 ≤ i ≤ g let H_{g+i} ⊆ A[p^n] be the i-th group of the Iwahori flag, locally (Z/p^nZ)^i in the interior. Let deg H_{g+i}(x) ∈ [0, ni] be its Fargues degree at a rank-one point x. Its degree divisor D_{H_{g+i}} = Z_i = D_{g+1} + ⋯ + D_{g+i} is cut out locally on 𝔖^tor by a function f_{g+i}. Let w ∈ ^MW with sequence (w_1, …, w_g), where w_0 = 0 and w_i − w_{i−1} is 1 if g+i ∈ w^{−1}{g+1, …, 2g} and 0 otherwise (§3.1). Let ε ∈ Q. Define:
- 𝒰_{≥w−ε} = {x : |f_{g+i}|_x ≤ |p^{n(w_i−ε)}|_x for all i}, a quasi-compact open whose rank-one points satisfy deg H_{g+i}(x) ≥ n(w_i − ε) for all i;
- 𝒰_{≤w+ε}, the quasi-compact open whose rank-one points satisfy deg H_{g+i}(x) ≤ n(w_i + ε) for all i;
- 𝒵_{<w+ε} = closure of ∪_{ε′<ε} 𝒰_{≤w+ε′};
- 𝒵_{>w−ε} = closure of ∪_{ε′<ε} 𝒰_{≥w−ε′} (printed with ε′ > ε).
The last two are closed subsets with quasi-compact complements. Let t ∈ T^{++}(Q_p), i.e. t = diag(t_1, …, t_{2g}) with v(t_{g+1}) > ⋯ > v(t_{2g}) > v(t_1) > ⋯ > v(t_g). T denotes the Hecke correspondence C_t^tor with projections p_1, p_2, and T^t its transpose.

FL = P∖G → Spec Z_p is the flag variety, viewed as a scheme. For w ∈ ^MW:
- C_w = P∖PwB is the Bruhat cell;
- X_w = closure of C_w = ∪_{w′≤w} C_{w′};
- X^w = closure of P∖PwB^{op} (B^{op} the opposite Borel) is the opposite Schubert variety;
- Y_w = ∪_{w′≥w} C_{w′}, and there is an inclusion X^w ⊆ Y_w.
The Hodge–Tate period map gives a continuous map π_{HT,K_{p,n}} : |S^tor| → |FL|/K_{p,n} (§4.1.5). So every K_{p,n}-stable subset of FL pulls back to S^tor; examples are the tubes ]X_{w,F_p}[ and ]Y_{w,F_p}[ of the special fibres and their closures.

