[BP21] Lemma 3.5.10(5). Let K_p = K_{p,m′,b} with m′ ≥ b ≥ 0 and m′ > 0; the Iwahori subgroups K_{p,n} of this paper are the case m′ = n, b = 0. For t ∈ T^{++} and all m, n ∈ Z_{≥0},
]X_{w,k}[·(K_ptK_p)^m ∩ \overline{]Y_{w,k}[}·(K_pt^{−1}K_p)^n ⊆ ]C_{w,k}[_{m·min(t), \bar 0}K_p ∩ ]C_{w,k}[_{0, \overline{n·min(t)}}K_p.
Here \overline{]Y_{w,k}[} is the closure of the tube and min(t) = min_{α∈Φ^+} v(α(t)) > 0. The sets ]C_{w,k}[_{a,\bar b} and ]C_{w,k}[_{\bar a,b} are the partial closures of the neighbourhoods ]C_{w,k}[_{a,b} of the tube of the Bruhat cell ([BP21] §3.3.6). For t ∈ T^{++}(Q_p) one has min(t) ≥ 1, and these sets shrink as the indices grow. The paper therefore uses the lemma in the form T^m(π^{−1}_{HT,K_{p,n}}]X_{w,F_p}[) ∩ (T^t)^n(π^{−1}_{HT,K_{p,n}}\overline{]Y_{w,F_p}[}) ⊆ π^{−1}_{HT,K_{p,n}}(]C_{w,F_p}[_{m,\bar 0}K_{p,n} ∩ ]C_{w,F_p}[_{0,\bar n}K_{p,n}).

