§6.2.11, p. 63. The extension of ν_A to 𝔗_n for n large, i.e. the n-analyticity of a weight on a quasi-compact open of W, is planned: PadicMeasuresIwasawaAlgebras L0a ('with the prescribed analyticity condition for locally analytic weights') and OverconvergentAutomorphicForms O0 ('Prove a radius of analyticity locally uniformly in U'). Planned nowhere:
- the affinoid groups 𝒯_n and ℐ𝒲_n (the congruence subgroup of the Levi M reducing into M_{K_{p,P}});
- their formal models 𝔗_n and 𝔍𝔚_n, and the Borel subgroup 𝔅𝔍𝔚_n;
- the weight κ_A = −w_{0,M}w(ν_A + ρ) − ρ.

Remark 1.4.4 (p. 5) asserts this without proof: 'It holds if w = Id or w = w_0^M is the longest element of ^MW.' The item for Conjecture 5.6.1 mentions this claim only beside the conjecture, which must stay a Prop-valued statement. This item splits the proved cases off as a theorem.

The paper's own ingredients give the proof. By Remark 5.6.2 and the residual-fibre criterion (planned), it suffices to bound RΓ_w(K^pK_{w,p,P}, κ) ⊗ Z/pZ and its cuspidal version at Iwahori level, for every residual weight κ.
(1) For w = Id, the cuspidal complex lies in degree 0. Here D_{w,−} = ∅ (p. 58), so the complex is a direct summand of RΓ((Ig_{K^pK_{w,p,P}})_{Z/pZ}, ω^κ(−cusp)). The image of this Igusa variety in the minimal compactification is affine, and the higher direct images to the minimal compactification vanish ([Lan17], Thm. 8.6). This is the w = Id step on p. 58, and it does not use g = 2.
(2) For every w, both complexes have amplitude in [0, d], where d = g(g+1)/2. By Proposition 2.5.4 the residual complexes are direct summands of coherent cohomology on a closed subscheme of S^tor_{F_p}, which has dimension at most d. This gives the non-cuspidal bound for Id and the cuspidal bound for w_0^M.
(3) Take w = w_0^M = w_{0,M}w_0. Then w_{0,M}ww_0 = Id, so Theorem 5.5.2 gives M^•_{w_0^M} ≅ RHom_Λ(M^•_{Id,cusp}, Λ)[−d]. By (1) this has amplitude [d, d].

The residual-fibre criterion is planned. Every other input is missing:
- affineness of the ordinary locus in the minimal compactification;
- the relative vanishing;
- Proposition 2.5.4;
- Theorem 5.5.2.
